lly did get an evacuation  flight through uzbekistan her husband  farid decided to stay behind and help  others get out well i had a family there  i had  people i worked with and i couldn't  really leave them behind we both were in  state of  survivor's guilt thinking why  was i the privileged one  who managed to get in where there were  women with their small kids screaming  for help  infants left  unattended  small kids left unattended there  did you see these kids at the airport  yeah  were there a lot of them there were a  lot of them  with her own three children anxiously  waiting onai spent almost a week in  transit back to virginia farid who got a  more direct flight three days later was  there to welcome her i know  your family is afghan you grew up there  but did this experience at all make you  feel more american yes because the way i  was received here  as someone i belong  belonged to  and  the way they took care of me  everyone the soldiers the friends  the community itself  when you came home you felt like this is  your home this is my home  for cbs this morning saturday christina  raffini vienna virginia  you know that's just one story there are  so many others and there are still  people that are working  literally around the clock to try to  help those that are trying to get out be  it u.s citizen canadian citizen or  afghans that helped  in the u.s the reality is that so many  people that have been left behind will  have to remain behind and we'll have to  deal with this new power structure for  those who have left imagine growing up  somewhere and then yeah and realizing
 

